[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch4
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Layer-2 VLAN5 uplink on switch4 (subinterface eth0.5, member of bridge br_vlan5).  
Mechanism: PCs in VLAN5 can switch locally among access ports on switch4 (pc1↔pc2↔dc_pc5 pings succeed), but ARP to the default gateway 10.5.0.254 fails because the VLAN5 uplink towards the router-on-a-stick (sshswitch) is down. With no forwarding path out of switch4’s VLAN5 bridge, ARP requests never reach the gateway and remain “incomplete,” causing all off-switch traffic (including to 10.5.0.254) to fail.  
Localization: On switch4, ip -br link shows eth0.5@eth0 DOWN; bridge link shows “eth0.5 … state disabled” under br_vlan5. Meanwhile VLAN6’s uplink eth0.6 is UP and forwarding, which matches user reports that VLAN6 PCs are fine. On sshswitch, the gateway side is healthy: br_v5 is UP with 10.5.0.254/24 and eth0.5 is UP and in br_v5, confirming the break is on switch4. PCs’ ARP tables corroborate: pc1 and dc_pc5 show 10.5.0.254 as “incomplete.”  
Root cause: The VLAN5 uplink subinterface eth0.5 on switch4 is administratively down/disabled in the bridge, breaking VLAN5’s path to the gateway.  
Corrective fix: On switch4, bring up eth0.5 and ensure it’s enslaved to br_vlan5 (e.g., ip link set eth0.5 up; verify bridge port state is forwarding). Also persist the configuration so eth0.5 remains UP across reboots. Once eth0.5 forwards, ARP to 10.5.0.254 will resolve and VLAN5 gateway reachability will be restored.

=== END ===